body:
The test @thelfensdrfer provided is for key-value syntax. `false` is expected there and we're already handling it.

Original message is about `setValues` method where in case of failure it's expected to get array of keys.   @cebe the variant with array of values is [using a helper function](http://git.php.net/?p=pecl/caching/apc.git;a=blob;f=php_apc.c;h=9615039b396b9e7de983722f523b9eb7b9079322;hb=HEAD#l638) that loops through all the values, checks return and adds keys on failure http://git.php.net/?p=pecl/caching/apc.git;a=blob;f=php_apc.c;h=9615039b396b9e7de983722f523b9eb7b9079322;hb=HEAD#l663. Based on the code I see no reason why it should ever return false.

@cebe can you test the following situation with apc enabled/disabled in console:

``` php
$data = ['a' => 'b'];
$store = apc_store($data);
var_dump($store);

$fetch = apc_fetch(['a'], $ok);
var_dump($fetch);
var_dump($ok);
```

```
$ php -d apc.enable_cli=1 test.php 
array(0) {
}
array(1) {
  'a' =>
  string(1) "b"
}
bool(true)
```

```
$ php -d apc.enable_cli=0 test.php 
array(1) {
  'a' =>
  int(-1)
}
bool(false)
NULL
```
